00:49:15.050 --> 00:49:33.130
<v Ira Glass>Sandra Tsing Loh's story "Bad Sex with Bud Kemp" is not collected in her books Aliens in America, A Year in Van Nuys, or any of her fine books. She has been happily married for years to a man who is not in any way, any way, Bud Kemp. They live in Los Angeles.
<v Ira Glass>桑德拉·清·罗的故事《与巴德·肯普的糟糕性爱》并未收录在她的书籍《美国的异乡人》、《范奈斯的一年》或任何她的优秀作品中。她多年来一直与一位绝非巴德·肯普的男人幸福地生活在一起。他们住在洛杉矶。

00:49:33.130 --> 00:50:50.090
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "THE FIRST TIME" BY EYDIE GORME]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 伊迪·戈梅 (Eydie Gorme) 的《第一次》]

00:50:50.090 --> 00:50:53.300
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, When Businesses Act Like People.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，当企业像人类一样行事。

00:50:53.300 --> 00:51:02.670
<v Ira Glass>We have this case study from Alex Blumberg, a radio listener-- a radio listener like you, a radio listener like me-- here in Chicago.
<v Ira Glass>我们有这样一个来自广播听众亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg）的案例研究——一位像你一样的听众，一位像我一样的听众——这里在芝加哥。

00:51:02.670 --> 00:51:30.850
<v Alex Blumberg>This is a story about a first day that's in denial that it's a first day. The story starts when I was in my car. And I just realized that I have my preset station buttons arranged in order of preference. One, two, three, four, five, six. And so I sort of go down. And this station number six was the classic rock station, Rock 103. And number six is often just awful. Every once in a while, they'll have a good Led Zeppelin song on, and that's why I have it set.
<v Alex Blumberg>这是一个关于拒绝承认自己是“第一天”的第一天的故事。故事开始时我正开车。我意识到我把收音机预设频道按偏好顺序排列好了。一、二、三、四、五、六。所以我按顺序按。第六个频道是经典摇滚台，Rock 103。六号位通常烂透了。偶尔他们会放一首好的齐柏林飞艇（Led Zeppelin）的歌，所以我才设了它。

00:51:30.850 --> 00:51:50.420
<v Alex Blumberg>So I hit it, and there's that song, which I don't know the name of, but it's that one that goes, "ooh, child, things are going to get easier." And I think to myself, that's certainly an expansive definition of classic rock. So then I think, wow, this is great. And then another song comes on. And it's "Disco Inferno," or something like that.
<v Alex Blumberg>所以我按了它，传出了那首歌，我不知道名字，但就是那首唱着“噢，孩子，事情会变得更容易”的歌。我想，那对经典摇滚的定义可真是够宽泛的。然后我想，哇，这太棒了。然后又出来一首歌。是“迪斯科地狱”（Disco Inferno），或者诸如此类的。

00:51:50.420 --> 00:52:02.730
<v Alex Blumberg>And I think now, something must be wrong. So I look and I think, the first thing is that somebody's been messing with my dials. Like somebody got into my car and rearranged my radio stations. But then I look at the numbers. The numbers are the same.
<v Alex Blumberg>我心想，一定是哪里出错了。所以我看看，心想，第一件事就是有人动了我的旋钮。就像有人钻进我的车里重排了我的广播频道。但接着我看了看数字。数字是一样的。

00:52:02.730 --> 00:52:34.450
<v Alex Blumberg>And then I hear this announcement that comes on. And it's like, "You've found the new 1035, Jammin' Oldies." And I think to myself, what the hell is a jammin' oldie? And you have to keep in mind that the day before, this station had been playing Anthrax and Slayer, and every once in a while, like I said, a Led Zeppelin song, but basically heavy metal gearhead music. And now all of a sudden, there's just no mention being made of it. And it's just like the old station was disappeared.
<v Alex Blumberg>然后我听到了这样的广播通告。就像，“你找到了全新的103.5，Jammin' Oldies（劲爆老歌）。”我心想，这玩意儿到底什么是“劲爆老歌”？你必须记住，前一天，这个电台还在放Anthrax和Slayer（金属乐队），就像我说的，偶尔放一首齐柏林飞艇的歌，但基本上是重金属齿轮发烧友的音乐。而现在突然之间，完全没有提及那件事。就像旧电台被抹掉了一样。

00:52:34.450 --> 00:52:45.590
<v Announcer>You've found the new 1035. Chicago's new station for jammin' oldies. We don't play moldy oldies, and we don't play doo-wop--
<v Announcer>你找到了全新的103.5。芝加哥播放劲爆老歌的全新电台。我们不播霉变的老歌，也不播杜沃普（doo-wop）——

00:52:45.590 --> 00:53:06.190
<v Alex Blumberg>Now what has happened is that this station had a format change over the weekend, basically. And they didn't mention it on the weekend, when I had been listening before. There was no mention of it. They were doing their concert promotions and having people call up to get Anthrax tickets or whatever. Nothing had been said.
<v Alex Blumberg>现在发生的情况是，这个电台基本上在周末改了台风。他们周末的时候提都没提，那时我还听着呢。完全没有提及。他们当时还在做演唱会推广，让人们打电话赢取Anthrax的票什么的。什么都没说。

00:53:06.190 --> 00:53:15.970
<v Alex Blumberg>And so obviously, everybody who has been tuning in before is expecting the classic rock. But they don't want to seem like they're a new station. They don't want to seem like the new kid on the block.
<v Alex Blumberg>所以显然，所有之前一直在收听的人都期待着经典摇滚。但他们不想表现得像个新电台。他们不想看起来像个新来乍到的家伙。

00:53:15.970 --> 00:53:44.740
<v Alex Blumberg>So what they do, is they do what everybody does on the first day. They pretend like it's not the first day. They want to establish this illusion that they've been around forever, so they have these little promo spots that come on, these little [UNINTELLIGIBLE] where they have people they've interviewed-- like, man on the street interviews-- where people are talking about how great jammin' oldies are, and how wonderful it is to have this station that plays jammin' oldies which has been on the radio for three hours.
<v Alex Blumberg>所以他们怎么做呢？他们像任何人在第一天做的那样。他们假装这不是第一天。他们想要建立一种仿佛已经存在了很久的错觉，所以他们做了这些小广告，这些[不明]，采访了一些人——就像街头采访——人们谈论着劲爆老歌有多棒，拥有这个已经开播了三小时的劲爆老歌电台有多美妙。

00:53:44.740 --> 00:53:55.120
<v Announcer 2>Everybody's talking about the new station that plays Chicago's jammin' oldies.
<v Announcer 2>每个人都在谈论这个播放芝加哥劲爆老歌的新电台。

00:53:55.120 --> 00:53:59.430
<v Woman>You know, when you hear those songs, it brings you back, like you're right there.
<v Woman>你知道，当你听到那些歌时，它带你回到了过去，就像你就身临其境一样。

00:53:59.430 --> 00:54:08.930
<v Alex Blumberg>You know? Like, what did they do? They just start broadcasting, and immediately send a squad of people out to find people who are accidentally tuned in? No.
<v Alex Blumberg>你知道吗？他们做了什么？他们就直接开始广播，然后立刻派出一队人去寻找偶然调频到这的人吗？没有。

00:54:08.930 --> 00:54:30.560
<v Alex Blumberg>And that also comes through. I mean, if you hear the spots, it's like this sort of desperately-- It's like they're trying too hard. And it's just exactly what happens when you're trying to fit in too hard, and you're pretending like you've around forever, and you know the ropes, and it just falls flat. And these spots just fall flat.
<v Alex Blumberg>这也能听得出来。我的意思是，如果你听那些广告，就像是一种绝望的……就像他们太努力了。这完全就是当你拼命想要融入、假装自己在这儿待很久了、知道门道时发生的事，结果却适得其反。这些广告听起来就很生硬。

00:54:30.560 --> 00:54:40.130
<v Alex Blumberg>The one that I love is where they have, like, "This is the station that plays jammin' oldies." And then they cut to this voice of somebody going, "jammin' oldies!"
<v Alex Blumberg>我最喜欢的一段是他们说：“这是播放劲爆老歌的电台。”然后切到一个人的声音喊道：“劲爆老歌！”

00:54:40.130 --> 00:54:43.480
<v Announcer 1>Now here comes more of Chicago's jammin' oldies.
<v Announcer 1>现在带来更多芝加哥的劲爆老歌。

00:54:43.480 --> 00:54:44.440
<v Excited Woman>Jammin' oldies!
<v Excited Woman>劲爆老歌！

00:54:44.440 --> 00:54:46.120
<v Announcer 1>On the new 103--
<v Announcer 1>在全新的103——

00:54:46.120 --> 00:55:27.810
<v Alex Blumberg>I don't quite understand what it is that is just so ridiculous about that particular "jammin' oldies." But it's just like it's so earnest and so wrong. It's just not right. It's just not what somebody would say if they were actually excited about jammin' oldies. They would never-- I can't imagine a situation in which somebody would authentically be shouting "jammin' oldies! Jammin' oldies!" A jammin' oldies pep rally? I can't imagine where it would be happening.
<v Alex Blumberg>我不太理解那种特定的“劲爆老歌”到底哪里那么荒谬。但听起来就是那么诚恳又那么不对劲。简直太不对了。如果有人真的对劲爆老歌感到兴奋，他们绝对不会那样说。我无法想象有什么情况下会有人真正发自内心地大喊“劲爆老歌！劲爆老歌！”劲爆老歌动员大会吗？我无法想象那会在哪儿发生。

00:55:27.810 --> 00:56:17.500
<v Alex Blumberg>I mean, I have nothing in common with the station formerly known as Rock 103. And yet, all the behaviors that they are exhibiting on their first day are behaviors that I would exhibit on my first day anywhere. Like they are trying to fit in, they're expressing sort of this anxious enthusiasm, the false bravado. They're pretending to have experienced things that they have, in fact, not experienced. And they're failing miserably, just as I would fail miserably, and have, on many occasions, on my first day.
<v Alex Blumberg>我是说，我和那个前身为Rock 103的电台没有任何共同点。然而，他们在第一天表现出的所有行为，都是我会在任何地方的第一天表现出的行为。比如他们试图融入，他们表现出一种焦虑的热情、虚假的虚张声势。他们假装经历了他们实际上从未经历过的事情。而且他们惨败了，就像我在很多场合的第一天也会惨败一样。

00:56:17.500 --> 00:56:46.790
<v Ira Glass>Alex Blumberg.
<v Ira Glass>亚历克斯·布隆伯格。

00:56:46.790 --> 00:57:11.850
<v Ira Glass>Well, it's This American Life , the radio program that can not resist a dare. Our program was produced today by Nancy Updike and myself with Alix Spiegel, Julie Snyder, and Emi Takahara. Our staff for this show includes Alex Blumberg, Susan Burton, Blue Chevigny, Todd Bachmann, and Starlee Kine. Contributing editors Paul Tough, Jack Hitt, Margy Rochlin, Alix Spiegel, and Consigliere Sarah Vowell. Marketing help from [? Marge Estrusco ?] and the friendly folks at PRI. Elizabeth Meister runs our website.
<v Ira Glass>好了，这里是《美国生活》，那个无法抗拒挑战的广播节目。我们今天的节目由南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）和我本人制作，联合制作人还有阿里克斯·斯皮格尔（Alix Spiegel）、朱莉·斯奈德（Julie Snyder）和艾米·高原（Emi Takahara）。节目组还包括亚历克斯·布隆伯格、苏珊·伯顿（Susan Burton）、蓝色·切维尼（Blue Chevigny）、托德·巴赫曼（Todd Bachmann）和斯塔莉·凯恩（Starlee Kine）。特约编辑保罗·塔夫（Paul Tough）、杰克·希特（Jack Hitt）、玛吉·罗克林（Margy Rochlin）、阿里克斯·斯皮格尔和顾问莎拉·沃威尔（Sarah Vowell）。市场营销帮助来自[？玛吉·埃斯特鲁斯科]和国际公共广播电台（PRI）的朋友们。伊丽莎白·迈斯特（Elizabeth Meister）负责我们的网站。

00:57:11.850 --> 00:57:18.830
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[鸣谢]

00:57:18.830 --> 00:57:24.760
<v Ira Glass>We've been meaning to thank Jorge Just for a few weeks now, so let's do it now. Thanks, J.
<v Ira Glass>我们一直想感谢豪尔赫·贾斯特（Jorge Just），几周了，现在就感谢一下。谢谢你，J。

00:57:24.760 --> 00:57:42.990
